  "title": "Rival colonies, switched on in the bed you play",
  "question": "This is now what a new box does by default. Is that the bed you want to open - and can you see a colony losing, or does it just quietly have fewer ants at the end?",
  "context_md": "**You said ship it on, so it is on.** `ant.ron` now authors `scent_spread: 2.0`. Two clicks found two families instead of one, and they treat each other as strangers - which in this engine means each is *food* to the other's ordinary mouth before it is ever a target for the fight verb.\n\n**Both strips are the same bed, same seed, same 4,200 frames, same ground.** A is the bed as it was yesterday; B is the bed a new box gives you today.\n\n- **A** - before. **0 killings between colonies.**\n- **B** - now, by default. **9 killings between colonies.**\n\n**Why 2.0 and not 1.0.** The dial has to clear a recognition radius of 1.0 before two colonies are strangers at all, and it is a *draw* - so it works on some world seeds and not others. Over 12 seeds: at 1.0, 9 of 12 beds show a killing; at 2.0, 11 of 12; at 3.0, still 11 of 12 and the worst seed gets worse. So 2.0 is where it becomes reliable rather than where it becomes fierce.\n\n**What it costs, same seeds, paired**: about 98 more deaths per run (up on 11 of 12 seeds), and starvation drops ~4.6 points as a share - the killing *displaces* starving rather than adding to it. Population is unmoved. The bed carries it.\n\n**Two things I did not do, and would rather you ruled on.** One bed in twelve still never separates at any setting - the signature is clamped to a cube and two colonies can land in the same corner. And the other five foundable ants in the held world (HOPPER, LONG ANT, SEGMENTED, BROAD, PALE CHITIN) are still one family with themselves; only COMMON ANT is switched on. Set it to 0 anywhere to get yesterday's bed back exactly.",
